I've written two task files and the issue-responses file in session_plan/. The plan commit didn't happen: session_plan/ is gitignored, so git add refused. The suggested command ends in || true, so I assume the harness reads the files from disk.

Task 1 — Part of #1250 (build): the model can call a named agent, and the trust gate ships in the same commit.
• The tool: a new agent tool takes subagent_type and prompt, and its name goes into BUILTIN_TOOL_NAMES.
• The gate: a pure function decides who can be called.
  • Agents from the user's home folder can be called.
  • Agents from the project folder can be called only when is_trust_project() is true; otherwise the call is refused with the same remedy project skills print.
  • An agent whose tool list resolves to nothing is refused too.
• What the model sees: the tool description lists only the agents it may call, with name and description, capped in size at a safe character boundary. No body text appears there, and a test checks that with a marker string.
• Who gets the tool: it is registered only when at least one agent can be called, so a user with no agent files sees the same tool list as today.
• The child agent's tools go through the existing sub_agent_child_tools_with path, so deny rules, folder restrictions and read-only mode still apply.
• File sizes: all logic and tests go in a new src/agent_dispatch.rs. agent_builder.rs and tools.rs are both exactly at their recorded size limit, so the edit to agent_builder.rs stays around 10 lines.
• Order of work: post an "in progress" comment on #1250 first and commit at the first green build. The positive control on the gate runs as one command that breaks it, runs the tests and restores it.

Task 2 — Part of #1250 (fix): agent discovery searches subfolders, as Claude Code does. Today a file like .claude/agents/review/x.md is neither found nor reported as skipped.
• The search goes at most 8 levels deep, and deeper folders are reported as skipped.
• Symlinked folders are not entered, and are reported.
• Files are visited in sorted order, so results are the same every run.
• When two files share a name, today's rule is reused.
• Every "not recursive" claim in src/ and the docs gets replaced.

